body:
> @Alex2782 would you be able to test the lastest changes on Android with BT mouse?

I will try to find time later to investigate the problems more closely.

------

I believe we can ignore `DEVICE_ID_EMULATION` for multitouch; will that make it simpler? 
And is the workaround with the value `-3` still necessary then?

```cpp
		} else if (touch->get_index() == status.touch_index) {
			if (!touch->is_pressed()) {
				status.touch_index = -1;
				status.device_id = -3;
				// problem between mouse (touch simulation) and touch
				// For mouse it should still be hovering when the position is on the button
				// For touch it should not be hovering since the finger is up
				// status.hovering = has_point(touch->get_position());
				status.hovering = false;
				on_action_event(p_event);
			}
		}
```

If `-3` is truly necessary, then it should be inserted here as a constant to avoid future conflicts.

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/a8e37fc01048823f639c4011af645d2e8e2b79d3/core/input/input_event.h#L63-L67

I've tested it on macOS so far. `OptionButton` doesn't work, the list of values ​​no longer opens. 
Can you reproduce this?

------

In theory, `BaseButton` should work everywhere without `Emulate Mouse from Touch` and `Emulate Touch from Mouse`. 
I think that's the minimum requirement to eventually remove `TouchScreenButton`.

```cpp
void BaseButton::gui_input(const Ref<InputEvent> &p_event) {
	ERR_FAIL_COND(p_event.is_null());

	if (status.disabled) { // no interaction with disabled button
		return;
	}

	if (p_event->get_device() == InputEvent::DEVICE_ID_EMULATION) {
		return; // Users could disable "Emulate Mouse from Touch" / "Emulate Touch from Mouse"; it should also work without emulation.
	}
```

I'll try my luck too, probably again from Monday onwards, if you haven't found a solution by then.

<details><summary>some notes on "DEVICE_ID_EMULATION"</summary>

-------

I only see this helper function that emulates the left mouse button. Introduced 6 years ago, Godot 3.1. Probably not important for buttons; the comment mentions `window`.

```cpp
void SceneTree::_main_window_focus_in() {
	Input *id = Input::get_singleton();
	if (id) {
		id->ensure_touch_mouse_raised();
	}
}

......

// Calling this whenever the game window is focused helps unsticking the "touch mouse"
// if the OS or its abstraction class hasn't properly reported that touch pointers raised
void Input::ensure_touch_mouse_raised() {
    ....
    button_event->set_device(InputEvent::DEVICE_ID_EMULATION);


```
